!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/core.tn?sha256=4e8d9c43cbbabf6a73548b819ac74e06e18859b573d05ce99988ae9d4d9a562e"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/numeric.tn?sha256=dfa66c5d3775a7845776db737f07b16630824f2434f6587aface2f88b77bf0f2"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/temporal.tn?sha256=fb4ada8f238de82821e7fcf512c21980a8b73c8df9260cdf295ba0157cc3971e"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/net.tn?sha256=371fc20c8628b5461b56a7a28f2559a1cfde4eda718f72d60ceb1b5a007bac06"
@doc:"""
  TSON core — every bundled type in one import (2026 Revision 39 draft).

  core.tn imports the three data-format libraries, numeric.tn, temporal.tn
  and net.tn, each of which imports the kernel, meta.tn, alone. A schema
  importing core therefore has the whole bundled vocabulary, each type under
  one identity ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3): the common types at the roots of the
  kernel and the libraries (`text`, `integer`, `bytes`, `enum`, `set`, `doc`,
  `number`, `int32`, `date`, `datetime`, `uuid`, `uri`), and the machinery
  under the maps those documents declare, by path (`meta.record`,
  `meta.type_ref`, `unicode.text_type`, `numeric.decimal_type`,
  `temporal.date_type`, `net.uuid_type`). It reserves the map names `meta`,
  `atom`, `numeric`, `unicode`, `net`, `binary` and `temporal` and every root
  name of those documents and of this one. A schema that wants only some of
  the libraries imports those instead.

  Its own root holds the author annotations, which describe a declaration or
  a field for its readers and tools and never change what it admits:
  `@deprecated`, `@title`, `@comment`, `@examples`, `@read_only` and
  `@write_only`. It declares no namespace of its own.

  Hash pins here are real digests over the bytes of this copy; see meta.tn's
  note.

  References:
    JSON Schema 2020-12   Meta-data and annotation vocabulary (`title`,
                          `deprecated`, `readOnly`, `writeOnly`,
                          `examples`, `$comment`)
  """
!schema {
  @doc:"""
    Marks a definition or field as deprecated. A bare presence marker like
    `@atom.numeric`; the reason, where there is one, is `@doc`'s to give.
    """
  deprecated => @meta.annotation void

  @doc:"Short human-readable name for a definition or field."
  title => @meta.annotation text

  @doc:"""
    A note for a schema's maintainers rather than its readers (JSON Schema's
    `$comment`): where `@doc` is what a documentation tool shows, `@comment`
    is what it leaves out.
    """
  comment => @meta.annotation text

  @doc:"""
    Example values for a definition or field, each the text a reader is shown:
    conventionally the value written in TSON notation, so a record's example
    is a string such as `"{ x: 1 }"`. Nothing parses or checks an example
    against the type it illustrates; the annotation is documentation only.
    """
  examples => @meta.annotation [text]

  @doc:"""
    Marks a field an encoding's consumers may read but not write. A bare
    presence marker like `@atom.numeric`: absence is the default, so there is no
    false to write (JSON Schema's `readOnly: false` maps to no annotation).
    """
  read_only => @meta.annotation void

  @doc:"""
    Marks a field an encoding's consumers may write but not read. A bare
    presence marker, on `@read_only`'s terms.
    """
  write_only => @meta.annotation void
}
